    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe three files touched by the diff are individually clean and idiomatic: `HouseholdDuplicateException` mirrors the sibling service exceptions, its handler in `ExceptionControllerAdvice` is a byte-for-byte match of the existing `DuplicateIdentityException`/`CityOwnerLimitException` handlers (right placement among the 409s, right logging, right `detailBuild`), the two new service methods (`assignHouseholdId`, `assertHouseholdUnique`) are well-scoped and correctly named, the now-unused helpers (`findHouseholdMembers`, `assignHousehold`, `resolveHouseholdId`, `normalizeHouseholdAddress`) and imports (`ArrayList`, `Objects`) were properly pruned, and the OpenAPI docs were updated to match. On the changed files alone this is exemplary.\n\nThe one structural problem is what the diff leaves behind, not what it adds:\n\n- **`Owner.possibleDuplicate` / `possibleDuplicateOf` are now orphaned, write-dead state.** The removed `markPossibleDuplicate` was the *only* writer of these two fields anywhere in production code (confirmed: after this diff they appear solely in `Owner.java` getters/setters and `openapi.yml`). `assertHouseholdUnique` replaced soft-flagging with a hard 409, so nothing ever calls `setPossibleDuplicate(...)` or `setPossibleDuplicateOf(...)` again. A maintainer landing on `Owner` will find two fields (plus their OpenAPI `possibleDuplicate`/`possibleDuplicateOf` schema entries, whose descriptions still reference \"lastName and postcode \u2026 set when possibleDuplicate is true\") that no code path can ever populate \u2014 vestigial model state directly created by this change.\n\nActionable: as part of this change, either remove `possibleDuplicate`/`possibleDuplicateOf` from `Owner` and their `openapi.yml` schema entries so the model reflects the new household-keyed rule, or, if they must be retained for API compatibility, add a note explaining why they are intentionally left unwritten. Right now the change silently strands them.",
